				\begin{document}$$\begin{aligned} {\left\{ \begin{array}{ll} \begin{aligned} Accuracy & = \frac{TP+TN}{TP+FP+TN+FN} \\ Precision & = \frac{TP}{TP+FP} \\ Recall & = \frac{TP}{TP+FN} \\ F_1score & = \frac{(1+\beta ^2)\times \text {Precision}\times \text {Recall}}{\beta ^2\times \text {Precision}+\text {Recall}} \\ MCC & = \frac{TP\times TN-FP\times FN}{\sqrt{(TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)}}\\ \end{aligned} \\ \end{array}\right. } \end{aligned}$$\end{document}Accuracy=TP+TNTP+FP+TN+FNPrecision=TPTP+FPRecall=TPTP+FNF1score=(1+β2)×Precision×Recallβ2×Precision+RecallMCC=TP×TN-FP×FN(TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)where TP, FP, TN and FN are the number of true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives. MCC provides a balanced assessment of a model’s overall classification performance, which is especially valuable in scenarios with imbalanced class distribution [49]. In addition, Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC) and Average Precision (AP) were used, which are defined as the area under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) and Precision-Recall (PR) curve.

During training, we set 20% of the training data for validation. At each iteration epoch of the model, we assessed its performance on the validation set, with the MCC serving as the primary evaluation metric. It is important to note that all models shared identical training conditions, including the selection of the validation set (same random seed) and the configuration of hyperparameters (batch size=256, lr=\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
